    <mytitle|Where we are>

    \;

    <with|font-series|bold|Lecture 4.> Interacting field at resolution
    <math|t> = solution of the FBSDE

    <\equation*>
      \<Phi\><rsub|t>=<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|t><wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|s>*F<rsub|s><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>s+<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|t><wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|s><rsup|1/2>\<mathd\>W<rsub|s>,<space|2em>F<rsub|t>=\<bbb-E\><rsub|t>*<around*|[|-D*V<rsub|T><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|T>|)>|]>.
    </equation*>

    In the <strong|forecast coordinate> <math|\<psi\><rsub|t>=\<bbb-E\><rsub|t>*\<Phi\><rsub|T>>
    the forward equation has no drift and the trees are integrated exactly;
    the backward half is the <strong|Wetterich equation>.

    <with|font-series|bold|Lectures 5\U7.> Construct
    <math|\<Phi\><rsup|4><rsub|3>> on <math|\<bbb-T\><rsup|3>> with this
    FBSDE:

    <\enumerate>
      <item><strong|deterministic renormalisation>: an explicit approximate
      effective action <math|<wide|\<Gamma\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q>> and its defect
      (today);

      <item><strong|a priori estimates> for the exact FBSDE in the
      coordinates of <math|<wide|\<Gamma\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q>>, uniform in the
      lattice (Lecture 6);

      <item><strong|removing the cutoffs> and identifying the limit (Lecture
      7).
    </enumerate>

    <with|par-mode|right|<small|<with|color|dark grey|G., course notes on
    <math|\<Phi\><rsup|4><rsub|3>> via FBSDEs (2026); Bailleul\UChevyrev\UG.
    (in prep.)>>>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|What is proved>

    \;

    <\itemize>
      <item>Large mass <math|m<rsup|2>=q<rsub|0>> compared with the coupling:
      <math|\<gamma\>*q<rsub|0><rsup|-1/2>\<ll\>1>,
      <math|\<gamma\>=3*\<lambda\>>. A weak dimensionless coupling on a fixed
      torus.

      <item>Tightness of the lattice measures in <math|H<rsup|-s>>,
      <math|s\<gtr\><frac|1|2>>.

      <item>A subsequential limit: an FBSDE on
      <math|<around|[|q<rsub|0>,\<infty\>|)>>, driven by a Brownian motion
      obtained as the limit of the lattice innovations, whose terminal value
      has the limiting law.

      <item>A renormalised integration by parts formula.
    </itemize>

    <with|font-series|bold|Not proved:> uniqueness, OS axioms, infinite
    volume.

    <\myframed>
      <strong|The guiding principle>

      Do not estimate <math|\<Phi\>> and <math|F>. Estimate the
      <strong|error> of an explicit approximate forecast. UV stability
      <math|=> this error is small uniformly in the lattice.
    </myframed>

    <with|par-mode|right|<small|<with|color|dark grey|Other constructions:
    Glimm\UJaffe 1973; Feldman\UOsterwalder 1976; Brydges\UFröhlich\USokal
    1983; Barashkov\UG. 2020; G.\UHofmanová 2021>>>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <vspace*|2.5cm>

    \;

    <with|font-size|1.6|font-series|bold|color|dark red|The problem and the
    exact FBSDE>

    <vspace|0.3cm>

    <with|color|dark grey|Lecture 5>

    \;
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|Lattice and the mass-regulator profile>

    \;

    <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|N>=<around|(|N<rsup|-1>*\<bbb-Z\>/\<bbb-Z\>|)><rsup|3>>,
    <math|<big|int>f=N<rsup|-3>*<big|sum><rsub|x>f<around|(|x|)>>,
    <math|\<Delta\>=\<Delta\><rsub|N>> nearest neighbour. Resolution
    parameter <math|q=q<rsub|0>+t<rsup|2>\<in\><around|[|q<rsub|0>,\<infty\>|)>>:

    <\equation*>
      K<rsub|q>=q-\<Delta\>,<space|2em>C<rsub|q>=K<rsub|q><rsup|-1>,<space|2em>C=C<rsub|q<rsub|0>>=<around|(|m<rsup|2>-\<Delta\>|)><rsup|-1>.
    </equation*>

    In Lecture 3 notation (<math|T=\<infty\>>, and
    <math|<wide|Q|\<dot\>>=\<partial\><rsub|q>*Q<rsub|q>>):

    <\equation*>
      Q<rsub|q>=C-C<rsub|q>,<space|1em><wide|Q|\<dot\>>=C<rsub|q><rsup|2>,<space|1em><wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsup|1/2>=C<rsub|q>,<space|1em>S<rsub|q>=C<rsub|q>,<space|1em>\<cal-R\><rsub|q>=q-q<rsub|0>.
    </equation*>

    <\myframed>
      <strong|Free resolvents (uniform in <math|N>)>

      <math|C<rsub|q>\<ge\>0> as a kernel,
      <math|C<rsub|q>*<with|math-font|Bbb|1>=q<rsup|-1>*<with|math-font|Bbb|1>>,
      <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|C<rsub|q>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p\<to\>p>=q<rsup|-1>>,

      <\equation*>
        a<rsub|2><around|(|q|)>=<around|(|C<rsub|q><rsup|2>|)><around|(|x,x|)>\<lesssim\>q<rsup|-1/2>\<wedge\>N<rsup|3>*q<rsup|-2>,<space|2em><math-up|Tr>C\<asymp\>N.
      </equation*>
    </myframed>

    The <strong|bubble> <math|a<rsub|2><around|(|q|)>> is the
    coincident-point variance of the shell at resolution <math|q>.
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|The cutoff measure>

    \;

    <\equation*>
      U<around|(|\<varphi\>|)>=<frac|\<lambda\>|4>*<big|int>\<varphi\><rsup|4>,<space|2em>V<rsub|N>=U+<frac|\<mu\><rsub|N>|2>*<big|int>\<varphi\><rsup|2>,<space|2em>\<nu\><rsub|N><around|(|\<mathd\>\<varphi\>|)>=\<cal-Z\><rsub|N><rsup|-1>\<mathe\><rsup|-V<rsub|N><around|(|\<varphi\>|)>>\<mu\><rsup|C><around|(|\<mathd\>\<varphi\>|)>,
    </equation*>

    <math|\<gamma\>=3*\<lambda\>>, so <math|D<rsup|2>*U<around|(|y|)>=\<gamma\>*y<rsup|2>>
    (not the counterterm <math|\<gamma\><rsub|T>> of Lecture 4). The Wick
    ordering is now part of <math|\<mu\><rsub|N>>.

    <\theorem*>
      <dueto|informal>For <math|q<rsub|0>> large: explicit
      <math|\<mu\><rsub|N>=-3*\<lambda\><math-up|Tr>C+O<around|(|log N|)>>
      such that (a) <math|\<nu\><rsub|N>> is tight in <math|H<rsup|-s>>,
      <math|s\<gtr\><frac|1|2>>; (b) along a subsequence, the forecasts
      converge to a solution of a limiting FBSDE; (c) the limit satisfies a
      renormalised integration by parts formula.
    </theorem*>

    The bounds behind (a), (b) are bounds on the error <math|Z> of an
    approximate forecast, <strong|uniform in <math|N>>: UV stability.
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|The Polchinski process as a filtering problem>

    \;

    Fixed <math|N>. Hidden field <math|\<Phi\><rsub|\<infty\>>\<sim\>\<nu\><rsub|N>>,
    independent Brownian motion <math|B>, observations

    <\equation*>
      P<rsub|q>=<around|(|q-q<rsub|0>|)>*\<Phi\><rsub|\<infty\>>+B<rsub|q-q<rsub|0>>,<space|2em>\<cal-F\><rsub|q>=\<sigma\><around|(|P<rsub|s>:s\<le\>q|)>.
    </equation*>

    <\proposition*>
      With <math|\<cal-K\><rsub|q><around|(|p|)>=log
      <big|int>\<mathe\><rsup|<around|\<langle\>|p,\<varphi\>|\<rangle\>>-<frac|q-q<rsub|0>|2><around|\<\|\|\>|\<varphi\>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>>\<nu\><rsub|N><around|(|\<mathd\>\<varphi\>|)>>:

      <\itemize>
        <item>forecast <math|\<psi\><rsub|q>=\<bbb-E\><rsub|q>*\<Phi\><rsub|\<infty\>>=D*\<cal-K\><rsub|q><around|(|P<rsub|q>|)>>,
        covariance <math|<math-up|Cov><rsub|q>=D<rsup|2>*\<cal-K\><rsub|q><around|(|P<rsub|q>|)>\<gtr\>0>;

        <item>innovation <math|W<rsub|q>=P<rsub|q>-<big|int><rsub|q<rsub|0>><rsup|q>\<psi\><rsub|s>\<mathd\>s>
        is an <math|<around|(|\<cal-F\><rsub|q>|)>>-Brownian motion;

        <item><math|\<mathd\>\<psi\><rsub|q>=<math-up|Cov><rsub|q><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>W<rsub|q>>,
        and <math|\<partial\><rsub|q>*\<cal-K\>+<frac|1|2><math-up|Tr>D<rsup|2>*\<cal-K\>+<frac|1|2>*<around|\<\|\|\>|D*\<cal-K\>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>=0>.
      </itemize>
    </proposition*>

    Proof: Bayes with likelihood <math|exp
    <around|{|<around|\<langle\>|P<rsub|q>,\<varphi\>|\<rangle\>>-<frac|q-q<rsub|0>|2><around|\<\|\|\>|\<varphi\>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>|}>>;
    Lévy's characterisation; Itô on <math|D*\<cal-K\><rsub|q><around|(|P<rsub|q>|)>>.
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|<math|\<Phi\><rsub|q>=C<rsub|q>*P<rsub|q>> is the Polchinski
    field>

    \;

    <\proposition*>
      <math|\<Phi\><rsub|q>\<assign\>C<rsub|q>*P<rsub|q>> and
      <math|F<rsub|q>\<assign\>K<rsub|q>*<around|(|\<psi\><rsub|q>-\<Phi\><rsub|q>|)>>
      satisfy

      <\equation*>
        \<mathd\>\<Phi\><rsub|q>=C<rsub|q><rsup|2>*F<rsub|q><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>q+C<rsub|q><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>W<rsub|q>,<space|2em>F<rsub|q>=\<bbb-E\><rsub|q>*<around|[|-D*V<rsub|N><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|\<infty\>>|)>|]>,<space|2em>\<psi\><rsub|q>=\<Phi\><rsub|q>+C<rsub|q>*F<rsub|q>,
      </equation*>

      i.e. the FBSDE of Lecture 4 for the mass profile.
      <math|<math-up|Cov><rsub|q>> is the response
      <math|H<rsup|<math-up|ex>><rsub|q><around|(|\<psi\><rsub|q>|)>>.
    </proposition*>

    <\itemize>
      <item>Drift: <math|\<mathd\><around|(|C<rsub|q>*P<rsub|q>|)>=-C<rsub|q><rsup|2>*P<rsub|q>\<mathd\>q+C<rsub|q>*<around|(|\<psi\><rsub|q>\<mathd\>q+\<mathd\>W<rsub|q>|)>>.

      <item>Force: posterior integration by parts,
      <math|p=K<rsub|q>*D*\<cal-K\><rsub|q><around|(|p|)>+\<bbb-E\><rsub|<math-up|post>>*<around|[|D*V<rsub|N>|]>>
      (posterior with source <math|p>), at
      <math|p=K<rsub|q>*\<Phi\><rsub|q>>.

      <item>Same law as Lecture 4: both are the Gaussian law tilted by
      <math|\<mathe\><rsup|-V<rsub|N>>/\<cal-Z\><rsub|N>> of the terminal
      field; <math|\<sigma\><around|(|P|)>=\<sigma\><around|(|\<Phi\>|)>>.
    </itemize>

    <strong|At fixed <math|N> the FBSDE is a theorem>, and <math|W> is an
    innovation.
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|One site>

    \;

    <image|<tuple|<#>|onesite_filter.pdf>|1par|||>

    Illustration: <math|\<nu\>\<propto\>\<mathe\><rsup|-\<lambda\>*\<varphi\><rsup|4>/4>\<mu\><rsup|1/q<rsub|0>>>
    on one site, <math|\<lambda\>=2>, <math|q<rsub|0>=100>
    (<math|g<rsub|q<rsub|0>>=0.6>). <math|\<Phi\><rsub|q>=P<rsub|q>/q>, the
    posterior mean <math|\<psi\><rsub|q>>, and the approximate forecast
    <math|Y<rsub|q>> from <math|<wide|\<Gamma\>|\<bar\>>=<frac|\<lambda\>|4>*y<rsup|4>>.
    All converge to the hidden <math|\<Phi\><rsub|\<infty\>>>, and
    <math|q*Z<rsub|q>\<to\>0>.
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|Dictionary>

    \;

    \ <tabular*|<tformat|<cwith|1|-1|1|-1|cell-valign|c>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-tborder|1ln>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-bborder|1ln>|<cwith|7|7|1|-1|cell-bborder|1ln>|<table|<row|<cell|Lecture
    4>|<cell|filtering at resolution <math|q>>>|<row|<cell|field
    <math|\<Phi\><rsub|q>>>|<cell|<math|C<rsub|q>*P<rsub|q>>: posterior mean
    under the Gaussian prior <math|\<mu\><rsup|C>>>>|<row|<cell|forecast
    <math|\<psi\><rsub|q>>>|<cell|posterior mean
    <math|\<bbb-E\><rsub|q>*\<Phi\><rsub|\<infty\>>>>>|<row|<cell|response
    <math|H<rsup|<math-up|ex>><rsub|q><around|(|\<psi\><rsub|q>|)>=<math-up|Cov><rsub|q>>>|<cell|posterior
    covariance>>|<row|<cell|force <math|F<rsub|q>=K<rsub|q>*<around|(|\<psi\><rsub|q>-\<Phi\><rsub|q>|)>>>|<cell|posterior
    mean of <math|-D*V<rsub|N><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|\<infty\>>|)>>>>|<row|<cell|Brownian
    motion <math|W>>|<cell|innovation <math|P<rsub|q>-<big|int>\<psi\>>>>|<row|<cell|unresolved
    covariance <math|S<rsub|q>=C<rsub|q>>>|<cell|inverse of prior <math|+>
    observation precision>>>>>

    The lattice is the only UV cutoff: terminal resolution
    <math|q=\<infty\>>, and <math|\<Phi\><rsub|q>\<to\>\<Phi\><rsub|\<infty\>>>.
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|The Wetterich formulation>

    \;

    <math|\<Psi\><rsub|q>=\<cal-K\><rsub|q><rsup|\<ast\>>>,
    <space|1em><math|\<Gamma\><rsub|q><around|(|y|)>=\<Psi\><rsub|q><around|(|y|)>-<frac|1|2>*<around|\<langle\>|y,K<rsub|q>*y|\<rangle\>>>,
    <space|1em><math|H<rsup|<math-up|ex>><rsub|q><around|(|y|)>=<around|(|K<rsub|q>+D<rsup|2>*\<Gamma\><rsub|q><around|(|y|)>|)><rsup|-1>>.

    <\itemize>
      <item>Up to a function of <math|q>, <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|q>> is the
      <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|t>> of Lecture 4;
      <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|q>+<frac|1|2>*<around|\<langle\>|y,K<rsub|q<rsub|0>>*y|\<rangle\>>>
      is the effective average action with regulator
      <math|\<cal-R\><rsub|q>=q-q<rsub|0>>.

      <item>Coordinates: <math|\<Phi\><rsub|q>=\<psi\><rsub|q>+C<rsub|q>*D*\<Gamma\><rsub|q><around|(|\<psi\><rsub|q>|)>>
      (Lecture 4, <math|x=\<psi\>+S*<with|math-font|cal*|F><around|(|\<psi\>|)>>).
    </itemize>

    <\proposition*>
      <dueto|Wetterich with a mass regulator>

      <\equation*>
        \<partial\><rsub|q>*\<Gamma\><rsub|q><around|(|y|)>=<tfrac|1|2><math-up|Tr>H<rsup|<math-up|ex>><rsub|q><around|(|y|)>,<space|2em><math-up|Cov><rsub|q>=H<rsup|<math-up|ex>><rsub|q><around|(|\<psi\><rsub|q>|)>.
      </equation*>
    </proposition*>

    An <strong|identity about the unknown <math|\<nu\><rsub|N>>>, not a
    construction. Strategy: an approximate <math|<wide|\<Gamma\>|\<bar\>>>
    gives approximate forecast coordinates; the error should be driven by the
    failure of <math|<wide|\<Gamma\>|\<bar\>>> to solve Wetterich.
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|Approximate forecast and error>

    \;

    <math|<wide|\<Gamma\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q>> even,
    <math|<wide|\<Psi\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q>=<frac|1|2>*<around|\<langle\>|y,K<rsub|q>*y|\<rangle\>>+<wide|\<Gamma\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q>>
    uniformly convex, <math|<wide|\<cal-K\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q>=<wide|\<Psi\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q><rsup|\<ast\>>>.

    <\equation*>
      H<rsub|q><around|(|y|)>=<around|(|K<rsub|q>+D<rsup|2>*<wide|\<Gamma\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q><around|(|y|)>|)><rsup|-1>,<space|1em>e<rsub|q><around|(|y|)>=\<partial\><rsub|q>*<wide|\<Gamma\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q><around|(|y|)>-<tfrac|1|2><math-up|Tr><around*|[|H<rsub|q><around|(|y|)>-H<rsub|q><around|(|0|)>|]>,<space|1em>b<rsub|q>=-H<rsub|q>*D*e<rsub|q>.
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      Y<rsub|q>=D*<wide|\<cal-K\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q><around|(|P<rsub|q>|)><around|(|<text|approximate
      forecast>|)>,<space|2em>Z<rsub|q>=\<psi\><rsub|q>-Y<rsub|q><around|(|<text|error>|)>,<space|2em>\<Xi\><rsub|q>=<math-up|Cov><rsub|q>-H<rsub|q><around|(|Y<rsub|q>|)>.
    </equation*>

    <\theorem*>
      <dueto|exact, at fixed <math|N>>

      <\equation*>
        <\aligned>
          <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<mathd\>Y<rsub|q>>|<cell|=<around*|[|b<rsub|q><around|(|Y<rsub|q>|)>+H<rsub|q><around|(|Y<rsub|q>|)>*Z<rsub|q>|]>\<mathd\>q+H<rsub|q><around|(|Y<rsub|q>|)><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>W<rsub|q>,>>|<row|<cell|\<mathd\>Z<rsub|q>>|<cell|=-<around*|[|b<rsub|q><around|(|Y<rsub|q>|)>+H<rsub|q><around|(|Y<rsub|q>|)>*Z<rsub|q>|]>\<mathd\>q+\<Xi\><rsub|q><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>W<rsub|q>,>>>>
        </aligned>

        <space|2em>Y<rsub|q<rsub|0>>=Z<rsub|q<rsub|0>>=0.
      </equation*>
    </theorem*>

    Proof: <math|\<partial\><rsub|q>*<wide|\<cal-K\>|\<bar\>>+<frac|1|2><math-up|Tr>D<rsup|2>*<wide|\<cal-K\>|\<bar\>>+<frac|1|2>*<around|\<\|\|\>|D*<wide|\<cal-K\>|\<bar\>>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>=-e\<circ\>D*<wide|\<cal-K\>|\<bar\>>+<frac|1|2><math-up|Tr>H<around|(|0|)>>;
    differentiate in <math|p>, Itô along <math|\<mathd\>P=\<psi\><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>q+\<mathd\>W>.
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|Reading the system>

    \;

    <\itemize>
      <item><math|Y>: forward and explicit once <math|Z> is known;
      coefficients <math|b,H> built from <math|<wide|\<Gamma\>|\<bar\>>>.

      <item><math|Z>: backward. The terminal condition
      <math|q*Z<rsub|q>\<to\>0> is <strong|proved> at fixed <math|N> (Lecture
      7), because <math|<wide|\<Gamma\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q>\<to\>> bare action.

      <item>The only source of <math|Z> is the drift <math|b>, the gradient
      of the defect, transported by <math|exp <big|int>H>.
    </itemize>

    <\myframed>
      <strong|UV stability>

      The explicit action <math|<wide|\<Gamma\>|\<bar\>>> computes the
      forecast up to an error <math|Z> which is small uniformly in <math|N>.
    </myframed>

    Needed: (i) a defect integrable against the backward propagator
    (<strong|renormalisation>); (ii) global coercivity of
    <math|<wide|\<Gamma\>|\<bar\>>> (<strong|large fields>); (iii) a priori
    estimates (Lecture 6).
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|The structure of the construction>

    \;

    <with|par-mode|center|<\script-output|tikz|default>
      \\documentclass[tikz]{standalone}

      \\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}

      \\usepackage[sc]{mathpazo}

      \\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,mathtools,bm}

      \\usepackage[mathscr]{eucal}

      \\usetikzlibrary{decorations.pathreplacing,arrows.meta,positioning}

      \\usepackage[dvipsnames]{xcolor}

      \\definecolor{navy}{HTML}{1B2A41}

      \\definecolor{accent}{HTML}{B5402E}

      \\definecolor{SNavy}{HTML}{1B2A49}

      \\definecolor{STeal}{HTML}{1F7A72}

      \\definecolor{SGold}{HTML}{B8862B}

      \\begin{document}

      \\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.82,transform shape,

      \ \ box/.style={draw=navy,rounded corners=2pt,align=center,

      \ \ \ \ inner sep=4pt,font={},text width=4.0cm},

      \ \ arr/.style={-{Stealth[length=2.2mm]},thick,draw=navy!80}]

      \\node[box] (ren) at (0,0) {Renormalisation:\\\\ seed $+$ 2
      corrections,\\\\ mass path};

      \\node[box] (clip) at (5.4,0) {Clipping: global\\\\ coercivity,
      defect\\\\ $b=b^{\\mathrm b}+b^{\\mathrm l}$};

      \\node[box] (fbsde) at (10.8,0) {Exact FBSDE for\\\\ $(Y,Z)$ at fixed
      $N$};

      \\node[box] (fwd) at (2.2,-2.4) {Forward coercivity:\\\\ large fields
      $J$ by\\\\ Gaussian tails $+E$ (L6)};

      \\node[box] (bwd) at (8.6,-2.4) {Backward\\\\ representation:\\\\
      energy $E$ by $b^{\\mathrm b}$,\\\\ boundary,
      $\\frac{C\\gamma^2}{q_0}J$ (L6)};

      \\node[box] (bd) at (0,-4.8) {Boundary $qZ_q\\to0$\\\\ at fixed $N$
      (L7)};

      \\node[box] (uv) at (5.4,-4.8) {UV stability:\\\\
      $\\sup_N\\mathbb{E}\\\| Z_q\\\| ^2\\le Cq^{-5/2}$};

      \\node[box] (lim) at (10.8,-4.8) {Compactness, limit\\\\ FBSDE, DS
      (L7)};

      \\draw[arr] (ren)--(clip); \\draw[arr] (clip)--(fbsde);

      \\draw[arr] (fbsde)--(bwd); \\draw[arr] (clip)--(fwd);

      \\draw[arr] ([yshift=0.15cm]fwd.east) -- ([yshift=0.15cm]bwd.west);

      \\draw[arr] ([yshift=-0.15cm]bwd.west) -- ([yshift=-0.15cm]fwd.east);

